# =============================================================================
# LINE-ENDING POLICY: git must never rewrite a byte of this repository.
# =============================================================================
# This artifact ships byte-exact records. Every file was copied from the
# research repository and verified by sha256 against its source; a checkout
# that normalised line endings would silently defeat that guarantee while
# leaving the working-tree checksums of the *building* machine intact.
#
# The hazard is live, not theoretical: the machine this artifact was built on
# has core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM gitconfig (the Git-for-Windows default),
# so a plain `git clone` already exercises the hostile case. Of the 116 files
# copied from the research repository --
#     78 are CRLF-only, 37 are LF-only, and 1 is BOTH --
# so under normalisation the 37 LF-only files would be rewritten to CRLF on a
# Windows checkout and tests/test_clean.py would lose its 51 bare-LF lines.
# (Measured control: with this rule an LF-only file survives a clone byte-exact;
# without it, the same file comes back CRLF with a different sha256.)
#
# `* -text` disables ALL line-ending conversion for every path, in every
# clone, regardless of the cloner's core.autocrlf setting. It is the whole
# policy; the rule below is defence in depth.
* -text

# Named explicitly because it is the one known MIXED file: it begins with a
# UTF-8 BOM (ef bb bf) and carries 13,707 CRLF terminators alongside 51 bare
# LF ones. Redundant under `* -text` by design -- if that blanket rule is ever
# narrowed, this file must not be silently un-protected with it.
tests/test_clean.py -text

# Byte-exact data records: never diffed as text, never converted.
*.pt        binary
*.jsonl     -text -diff
